{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Your right hand, Your right hand.</b> Twice — when Israel do the will of the Omnipresent, the left becomes right (there)." "<b>Your right hand, YHWH, is glorious in power.</b> To save Israel, and Your right hand, a second time, shatters the enemy. And it appears to me that the right hand itself shatters the enemy — what is impossible for a person, to do two tasks with one hand. And the plain sense of the verse: Your right hand, which is glorious in power — what is its task? 'Your right hand, YHWH, shatters the enemy.' And how many verses are like it — ״כִּי הִנֵּה אוֹיְבֶיךָ ה׳ כִּי הִנֵּה אוֹיְבֶיךָ יֹאבֵדוּ״ (Psalms 92), and their like." "<b>Is glorious.</b> The yod is superfluous, like ״רַבָּתִי עָם שָׂרָתִי בַּמְּדִינוֹת״ (Lamentations 1), ״גְּנֻבְתִי יוֹם״ (Genesis 31)." "<b>Shatters the enemy.</b> It constantly shatters and breaks the enemy. And similar to it: ״וַיִּרְעֲצוּ וַיְרוֹצְצוּ אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, in Judges (10:8). (Another interpretation: Your right hand, which is glorious in power — it breaks and strikes the enemy.)"], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:20.728578322Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}